;; Representation. A `dyn` is a NaN-boxed JavaScript value; arithmetic runs on UNBOXED words, and
;; the backend chooses integer or floating instructions from the static type of its operands. A
;; `dyn` has no such type, so `(%Lt v 0)` on one compiles to a raw integer compare of a boxed word
;; — which is why a definition that reads correctly under the interpreter can be wrong natively.
;;
;; WHY THE UNBOXING IS WRITTEN HERE RATHER THAN HIDDEN IN THE BACKEND. `unbox-idealize` rewrites
;; `Unbox(Box(v))` to `v` whenever v's type is proven, so a chain of operations on values the
;; compiler can prove are integers loses ALL of its boxing. That only works while Box and Unbox are
;; nodes the optimiser can see. Move the unboxing into instruction selection and it happens after
;; the optimiser has run, so every intermediate is boxed and immediately unboxed forever, and the
;; cost is invisible in the source.
;;
;; The guard is the price. Where closed-world inference proves the type it folds away entirely;
;; where it cannot, a compare-and-branch remains.

;; The two unboxed arithmetic cores, one per representation. Each is written against a raw word, so
;; the backend selects integer or floating instructions from the parameter type alone.
(macro IntAbs :params [(x int)] :ret int (if (%Lt x 0) (%Sub 0 x) x))
(macro FltAbs :params [(x flt)] :ret flt (if (%Lt x 0) (%Sub 0 x) (%Add x 0)))
